/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 4.5 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: Contrast Low-Fidelity and High-Fidelity prototyping across cost, execution) Tj
T*
(velocity, user feedback depth, and design lifecycle stage.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Low-Fidelity prototypes \(paper sketches, wireframes, foam models\) are cheap,) Tj
T*
(created in minutes or hours, and used early in the design cycle. They encourage honest,) Tj
T*
(radical critique because users perceive them as incomplete works-in-progress, focusing) Tj
T*
(feedback on core concepts and workflow logic. High-Fidelity prototypes \(interactive) Tj
T*
(digital mockups, 3D functional models\) are expensive, require days or weeks, and appear) Tj
T*
(late in the cycle. Users focus on visual polish, typography, and micro-interactions,) Tj
T*
(making Hi-Fi ideal for usability testing, latency verification, and stakeholder) Tj
T*
(sign-off.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Define the Minimum Viable Product \(MVP\) and explain the operational difference) Tj
T*
(between a 'Wizard of Oz' MVP and a 'Concierge' MVP.) Tj
T*
(Answer: An MVP is the most stripped-down functional version of a product that enables a) Tj
T*
(team to collect validated empirical learning about customers with minimal effort. In a) Tj
T*
('Wizard of Oz' MVP, the front-end appears fully automated and intelligent to users, but) Tj
T*
(all background operations are performed manually by humans behind the scenes. In a) Tj
T*
('Concierge' MVP, the service is provided entirely manually and explicitly: the human) Tj
T*
(operator interacts directly with the customer without disguising the manual service,) Tj
T*
(allowing deep, bespoke qualitative observation of customer workflows before investing in) Tj
T*
(software automation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 4.6 Key Takeaways and Revision Points) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Prototypes are learning experiments designed to test specific hypotheses.) Tj
T*
(* Paper prototyping uncovers core navigational and mental-model flaws in hours.) Tj
T*
ET

